Demscore hex map — licensing
============================

This repository holds three kinds of thing, and they are not under the same
terms. Which one applies depends on what is being reused, not on which
directory it sits in.


1. Source code — MIT
--------------------

Everything this project wrote: `scripts/`, `sql/`, `web-backend/`,
`mapserver/`, `initdb/`, `router/`, `mapcache/`, `deploy/`, the compose and
deployment files, and the web app's own source under `webapp/` (a submodule,
github.com/humlab/demscore-web, which carries the same licence).

Copyright (c) 2025 Demscore / Humlab, Umeå University

Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy of
this software and associated documentation files (the "Software"), to deal in
the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights to
use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell copies
of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is furnished to do
so, subject to the following conditions:

The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all
copies or substantial portions of the Software.

THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED "AS IS", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR
IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,
FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE
AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER
LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM,
OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE
SOFTWARE.

The MIT grant covers the code as code. Running it against the boundary data
vendored here produces output that section 2 governs, and the code does not
license that output.


2. Map output and derived boundary data — CC BY-NC-SA 4.0
---------------------------------------------------------

The hexagon meshes (`demscore.hex_mesh`, `demscore.hex_mesh_mercator`), the
normalised country tables built from the vendored boundary sources
(`demscore.countries`, `demscore.historical_country_geometries_src` and the
border-edge tables derived from them), and the WMS tiles and map images
rendered from any of these.

These are adaptations of CShapes 2.0 and CShapes-Europe, which are licensed
CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. ShareAlike carries: the adaptations are offered onward under
the same licence.

    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/

In practice this means attribution (the sources are named in the app and in
`data/reference/README.md`), non-commercial use only, and the same licence on
anything derived further.

**This deployment is non-commercial.** No advertising, no paid access, no
bundling into a commercial service. That is the operating mode the boundary
sources require, not a preference. A deployment that needs an unencumbered map
would have to reduce `sql/historical_geometry.sql` to the Natural Earth rows and
lose every pre-1886 country — dropping a file is not enough, because the
CShapes-derived rows reach present-day years too (both Palestine units and
Zanzibar are drawn in 2020).

Some rendered views also contain polygons derived from historical-basemaps,
which is GPL-3.0. Five entities, ten hexagons at the 100 km size: Bavaria,
Hamburg, Hanover, Saxony and Tuscany. Its notice travels in the app's
"Sources & licences" dialog and in `data/reference/README.md`.


3. Vendored third-party data — each source's own licence
--------------------------------------------------------

Files under `data/` are not this project's to license. `data/reference/README.md`
is the register: Natural Earth (public domain), CShapes 2.0 and CShapes-Europe
(CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), historical-basemaps (GPL-3.0).

The indicator datasets (V-Dem, QoG, REPDEM), their codebook PDFs under
`data/Demscore_Module_Vars/` and `webapp/assets/codebooks/`, and the Demscore
wordmark belong to their respective providers. Each dataset requires citation as
a condition of use; the citations are in the app's "Sources & licences" dialog
and in `webapp/config/attribution_sources.json`.


Where the credits come from
---------------------------

The sources the app names are generated from the database rather than written by
hand: `scripts/build_attribution.sh` reads which geometry is actually drawn out
of `demscore.countries`, joins it to the register in
`webapp/config/attribution_sources.json`, and fails the build on a boundary
source it cannot name. A new source therefore reaches the credits, or it does
not ship.
